WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.717
The central finding is that sharing encoded
representations instead of raw features barely

00:00:03.797 --> 00:00:07.514
hurts predictive performance.

00:00:07.594 --> 00:00:11.311
On House Pricing,

00:00:11.391 --> 00:00:15.109
the raw-data baseline reaches a test R-squared of
about ninety percent,

00:00:15.189 --> 00:00:18.906
and the two-peer scenario using individual
autoencoders still reaches about eighty-nine percent,

00:00:18.986 --> 00:00:22.703
an almost negligible drop despite the data being
obfuscated.

00:00:22.783 --> 00:00:26.500
On Buzz in Social Media,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:30.297
the baseline R-squared is around ninety-six percent
and the encoded scenarios stay in

00:00:30.377 --> 00:00:34.094
the high eighties to low nineties.

00:00:34.174 --> 00:00:37.891
For MNIST digit classification,

00:00:37.971 --> 00:00:41.689
test accuracy moves from ninety-two percent at
baseline down to the mid-eighties under encoding.

00:00:41.769 --> 00:00:45.486
Importantly, the non-naive multitask scenarios,

00:00:45.566 --> 00:00:49.283
where the autoencoder is guided by the target
variable, recover much of this gap,

00:00:49.363 --> 00:00:53.080
showing that shaping the latent space with the
downstream task improves results.
